RDPServer 0.2-rc1 (release candidate)
=====================================

Lets you use this RISC OS desktop from another computer with Remote
Desktop: Windows' Remote Desktop Connection (mstsc), Microsoft's Windows
App on macOS, iOS and Android, FreeRDP or Remmina on Linux, and
!RDPClient on another RISC OS machine.

FOR YOUR OWN NETWORK ONLY. Its connections use RDP's older built-in
encryption (RC4), not TLS. Run it on your own network, behind your
router; never make port 3389 reachable from the internet. Set a login
(below) before you use it.

Needs: SharedUnixLibrary, ARMEABISupport and CryptRandom (PackMan), and a
desktop mode with thousands or millions of colours (16 or 32 bpp).

Using it
--------
Double-click !RDPServer. Its icon appears on the icon bar, and it
listens on port 3389. The first time, it makes this computer's key
(stored in Choices:RDPServer); that can take a minute, with the
hourglass showing. The icon changes while a client is connected.

  Select on the icon   the Status window: what it's doing, who's
                       connected, and its last few messages
  Adjust on the icon   Choices
  Menu on the icon     Info, Status..., Choices..., Disconnect, Quit

It uses almost no time while nothing is happening; while a client is
connected it looks for changes on the screen ten times a second.

The server itself is a module (RDPSMod, which !Run loads and Quit
removes), so it goes on working when the desktop doesn't multitask:
while an error box is open, the client still sees the screen (the box
included) and can click the box's buttons; a program that keeps the
machine to itself doesn't stop it either. If such a program is stopped
with Alt-Break while the server is in the middle of its turn, the server
starts again by itself, and a client that supports automatic
reconnection should connect again by itself within a few seconds,
without asking for the password (seen with Windows App, FreeRDP's
/auto-reconnect and rdesktop). It never holds the desktop up
for more than a couple of centiseconds at a time, also while a client is
connecting.

Disconnect (on the icon's menu) drops the client at once, even one that
is still connecting.

There is one client at a time, as RISC OS has one desktop and one user:
while a client is connected, another is turned away (the Log says so).
A client that connects but doesn't finish connecting within 30 seconds
is dropped, so it can't keep others out.

Setting a login
---------------
Until you set one, anyone who can reach the port can connect. In
Choices, type a user name and a password and click Save (or press
Return). Clients must then give them. To change the password, type a new
one; to let anyone connect again, click Clear login (it takes effect
straight away). The password isn't stored, only a salted SHA-256 of it
(Choices:RDPServer.logins). After a wrong password, new connections are
turned away for 3 seconds.

Choices also sets the port. Changing it disconnects a connected client.

Sound
-----
What this computer plays goes to the client too, if the client asks for
sound (Windows App, Remote Desktop Connection and FreeRDP do by default;
rdesktop only with -r sound). It's sent as it is, 16-bit stereo at the
computer's own sample rate (about 1.5 Mbit/s at 48 kHz), so it suits a
home network better than the internet. It reaches the client about a
third of a second late.

The computer goes on playing it too, unless you tick "Mute this computer
while a client listens" in Choices: it's then silent while a client
listens, and plays again when the client goes. The choice takes effect
at once, also while a client is connected.

If a program changes the sample rate while a client is listening, the
sound to that client stops until it connects again.

Connecting
----------
On the other computer, connect to this computer's IP address:
  Windows:   mstsc /v:192.168.1.50
             (if it asks, choose to connect anyway: the server's
             identity can't be checked without TLS)
  FreeRDP:   xfreerdp3 /v:192.168.1.50 /sec:rdp
  RDPClient: as for any server

The client gets the RISC OS screen at its own size. Mouse: left is
Select, middle is Menu, right is Adjust; the scroll wheel works where a
local USB mouse's wheel does. The keyboard works by key position, with
RISC OS's keyboard layout. Keys go to whichever window has the input
focus on RISC OS.

It writes a log, Choices:RDPServer.Log (and libxrdp's own, XrdpLog):
connections, sound, every mouse button and wheel event, and Shift, Ctrl
and Alt. Other keys are logged without saying which, so nothing you type
is kept.
A click goes to RISC OS when the client lets go of the button (or after
a quarter of a second, if you hold it, or as soon as you drag); the
button is then released 6 cs later by a ticker event, so double-clicks
reach the Filer as double-clicks.

What isn't done yet
-------------------
- TLS (needs AcornSSL to work as a server).
- Only changed areas are sent, but there's no bitmap cache yet, and no
  quick handling of window moves and scrolling.
- The clipboard, and file or printer sharing.
- H.264: video is sent as ordinary screen updates, so it uses a lot of
  the network and isn't smooth at large sizes.
- The pointer shape: the client shows its own arrow.
- Video and 3D shown in a hardware overlay (VideoOverlay, and EGL
  programs such as Reel, ReelEGL and Mesa games) appear on the client as
  a black window: the GPU draws overlays straight to the display, so they
  never reach screen memory where RDPServer reads the picture. They still
  show on this computer's own monitor. While a client is connected
  RDPServer sets EGL$Overlay to "off" (and puts your own setting back
  when the client goes) and RDPServer$Clients to 1, so programs can draw
  the ordinary way instead; Reel and the EGL library don't do that yet.
- Changing the screen mode while a client is connected resizes the
  client's desktop to the new mode; the client redraws its window as it
  does that, so it flashes once. A mode change at the same size (other
  colours, say) doesn't resize it, so doesn't flash. A client that
  can't be resized (it didn't say it could) is disconnected: connect
  again.

Reporting problems
------------------
Please say which client you used (and its version), what you did and
what happened, and attach Choices:RDPServer.Log and XrdpLog from that
run (they start afresh each time RDPServer starts). They hold no
passwords or typed text.
  https://github.com/adyoull/riscos-rdpserver/issues

Command line
------------
RDPServer -setlogin NAME PASSWORD
RDPServer -clearlogin
  set the one login clients must give, or let anyone connect (as
  Choices does)

